[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Closures and Anonymous Functions in PHP"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"PHP supports anonymous functions, also known as closures. These are functions without a specified name. They are often used as arguments to other functions, or as the return value of a function."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Anonymous Functions (Closures)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"An anonymous function is created using the 'function' keyword, but without providing a function name. It can be assigned to a variable, which can then be called like a regular function."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"&lt;?php
$greet = function($name) {
    return "Hello, " . $name . "!";
};

echo $greet('World'); // Output: Hello, World!

?&gt;"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, an anonymous function is assigned to the variable '$greet'. This function takes one argument, '$name', and returns a greeting string. The function is then called using '$greet('World')'."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Closures and Variable Scope"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Closures can 'capture' variables from the surrounding scope. By default, they do not have access to variables defined outside the function. To access these variables, you must use the 'use' keyword."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"&lt;?php
$message = 'Hello';

$greet = function($name) use ($message) {
    return $message . ', ' . $name . '!';
};

echo $greet('World'); // Output: Hello, World!

?&gt;"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the '$message' variable is captured from the surrounding scope using the 'use' keyword. The closure can then access and use this variable."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Passing Variables by Reference"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can also pass variables by reference to a closure. This means that any changes made to the variable inside the closure will affect the original variable."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"&lt;?php
$message = 'Hello';

$changeMessage = function() use (&$message) {
    $message = 'Goodbye';
};

echo $message . "\n"; // Output: Hello

$changeMessage();

echo $message . "\n"; // Output: Goodbye

?&gt;"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, '$message' is passed by reference using 'use (&$message)'. When '$changeMessage()' is called, it modifies the original '$message' variable."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Use Cases"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Callback functions: Anonymous functions are commonly used as callback functions, for example, with array functions like 'array_map', 'array_filter', and 'usort'."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Event handlers: They can be used to handle events in a more concise way."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Creating reusable code blocks: They allow you to define small, self-contained units of code that can be easily reused."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"&lt;?php
$numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];

$squared = array_map(function($n) {
    return $n * $n;
}, $numbers);

print_r($squared); // Output: Array ( [0] =&gt; 1 [1] =&gt; 4 [2] =&gt; 9 [3] =&gt; 16 [4] =&gt; 25 )

?&gt;"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example uses 'array_map' with an anonymous function to square each number in the '$numbers' array."}]
